1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,287
HBM, substrates, and bridges.

2
00:00:03,237 --> 00:00:06,887
This is episode five of From Sand to GPU.

3
00:00:06,887 --> 00:00:18,137
Advanced packaging only works when three distinct supply chains arrive on time: high-bandwidth memory stacks, an interposer or local silicon bridges, and a high-layer-count organic package substrate.

4
00:00:18,137 --> 00:00:20,362
Start with HBM.

5
00:00:20,362 --> 00:00:29,075
DRAM makers build three-D-stacked memory with a base die and through-silicon vias, sold as stacks that sit beside the GPU on the interposer or RDL.

6
00:00:29,075 --> 00:00:33,537
HBM3E was the volume workhorse into the mid-twenty-twenties.

7
00:00:33,537 --> 00:00:35,950
HBM4 is next.

8
00:00:35,950 --> 00:00:42,125
NVIDIA’s Rubin materials publicly pair the GPU with HBM4 — that pairing is confirmed.

9
00:00:42,125 --> 00:00:47,987
The three major vendors are SK hynix, Samsung, and Micron — confirmed as the industry set.

10
00:00:47,987 --> 00:00:52,725
Which vendor is qualified for which customer at which moment is time-varying.

11
00:00:52,725 --> 00:00:55,512
Next, the interconnect plane.

12
00:00:55,512 --> 00:01:01,725
CoWoS-S uses a large silicon interposer to redistribute signals between logic and HBM.

13
00:01:01,725 --> 00:01:11,525
CoWoS-L uses smaller local silicon interconnect bridges plus organic RDL to span multi-reticle assemblies — that description is likely per packaging analyses.

14
00:01:11,525 --> 00:01:17,412
Interposer area scales poorly, which is why CoWoS-L adoption narratives keep showing up.

15
00:01:17,412 --> 00:01:27,337
Beneath that assembly sits the organic package substrate — typically an advanced flip-chip BGA that uses Ajinomoto Build-up Film, or ABF, as the critical dielectric.

16
00:01:27,337 --> 00:01:35,437
Ajinomoto’s IR discusses ABF as interlayer insulating material for logic IC package substrates — confirmed product role.

17
00:01:35,437 --> 00:01:46,925
Substrate fabricators commonly named include Unimicron, Ibiden, Shinko, Nan Ya PCB, Samsung Electro-Mechanics, and AT and S — a likely leading set, not an exhaustive certified list.

18
00:01:46,925 --> 00:01:54,012
Materials dependencies stack quickly: HBM needs DRAM wafers, TSV, and bonding at the memory IDM.

19
00:01:54,012 --> 00:01:58,312
Interposers consume extra three-hundred-millimeter silicon.

20
00:01:58,312 --> 00:02:02,125
Bridges are small silicon dies with fine interconnect.

21
00:02:02,125 --> 00:02:05,325
ABF film comes from varnish to film.

22
00:02:05,325 --> 00:02:09,500
Copper, glass cloth, and laminate feed substrate makers.

23
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:13,125
Solder bumps and balls close the mechanical stack.

24
00:02:13,125 --> 00:02:15,275
Geography matters.

25
00:02:15,275 --> 00:02:19,900
HBM capacity is centered in Korea with mixed Micron footprints.

26
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:22,700
ABF film is Japan-centric.

27
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:27,237
Substrates cluster in Taiwan, Japan, Korea, and Europe.

28
00:02:27,237 --> 00:02:32,350
Interposer flow often sits inside TSMC’s packaging path in Taiwan.

29
00:02:32,350 --> 00:02:45,150
Public NVIDIA figures for Rubin-class packages cite up to two-hundred-eighty-eight gigabytes of HBM4 and twenty-two terabytes per second per GPU — confirmed from NVIDIA Developer Blog and NVL72 product tables.

30
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:53,975
Stack count is commonly discussed as eight stacks in secondary writeups — likely; confirm against official datasheet line items before locking a BOM quantity.

31
00:02:53,975 --> 00:03:01,012
Exact public split of Rubin HBM4 supply among the three vendors is unknown — contractual and rarely disclosed.

32
00:03:01,012 --> 00:03:07,275
Risks: HBM qualification is customer- and generation-specific, so dual-sourcing is slow.

33
00:03:07,275 --> 00:03:18,687
ABF and large substrates have been historical bottlenecks; twenty-twenty-six coverage describes renewed CapEx at major substrate makers — CapEx dollar figures in press are estimated unless primary.

34
00:03:18,687 --> 00:03:24,275
Substrate quality gates signal integrity for HBM and SerDes escaping the package.

35
00:03:24,275 --> 00:03:37,875
Trackers should watch HBM supplier earnings and sold-out commentary, NVIDIA’s three-vendor qualification health, Ajinomoto ABF CapEx, substrate lead times and layer counts for AI packages, and the HBM4 twelve-high versus eight-high mix.

36
00:03:37,875 --> 00:03:40,200
That is episode five.

37
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:45,912
Next time: assembly, test, and the rack — where a package becomes a shippable AI system.

